# Exclude all .tfvars files, which are likely to contain sensitive data, such as
# password, private keys, and other secrets. These should not be part of version
# control as they are data points which areIt is perfectly normal that the recipes aren't loading! When we sanitized `app.js` earlier, we replaced your actual Supabase and Gemini keys with dummy text. Right now, the frontend is basically an empty shell trying to talk to a backend that doesn't exist yet. We will wire all of that back up in Phase 2.

Before we move on, you are absolutely right: **do not commit yet.** 

If you run a standard `git add .` right now, you will accidentally commit the `terraform.tfstate` file. This file contains the exact blueprint of your cloud architecture in plain text, and committing it to a public GitHub repository is a major security flaw (and a fast way to fail an interview). You also don't want to commit the `.terraform/` folder, which is heavy and contains the downloaded AWS plugins.

Here is how to properly secure your Terraform project in version control.

### Step 1: Update your `.gitignore`
Open the `.gitignore` file located at the root of your project (outside the `infra` folder). Scroll to the very bottom, make a new line, and paste in the official HashiCorp Terraform ignore list:

```text
# =========================
# TERRAFORM IGNORE LIST
# =========================
# Local .terraform directories
**/.terraform/*

# .tfstate files (NEVER commit these!)
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# Crash log files
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Exclude all .tfvars files, which contain sensitive data
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json

# Ignore override files
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# Ignore transient lock info files created by terraform apply
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# Ignore CLI configuration files
.terraformrc
terraform.rc